<p><a name="footnote1"></a><sup>1</sup> I would like to thank Yan Chow, Jason  Miu, Matt Raskin, Tim Wessel, and Patricia Zobel for their assistance in  preparing these remarks; Steph Clampitt and David Tam for their assistance with  data; and colleagues in the Federal Reserve System for valuable comments and  suggestions.</p>
<p><a name="footnote2"></a><sup>2</sup> Logan, Lorie K. (2019). <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2019/log190417" target="_blank">Observations on Implementing Monetary  Policy in an Ample-Reserve Regime</a>, speech delivered at the Money Marketeers, April 17, 2019.</p>
<p><a name="footnote3"></a><sup>3</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcminutes20181219.pdf" target="_blank">Minutes of the Federal Open Market  Committee</a>,  December 18-19, 2018 and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcminutes20190130.pdf" target="_blank">Minutes of the Federal Open Market  Committee</a>, January  29-30, 2019. </p>
 <p><a name="footnote4"></a><sup>4</sup> For a discussion of the Desk&rsquo;s  actions to restore market functioning and support the flow of credit, please  see <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2020/log201023" target="_blank">Treasury Market Liquidity and Early  Lessons from the Pandemic Shock</a>, Lorie K. Logan, October 23, 2020; <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2020/log200715" target="_blank">The Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Market  Functioning Purchases: From Supporting to Sustaining</a>, Lorie K. Logan, July 15, 2020; and <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2020/log200414" target="_blank">The Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Recent Actions  to Support the Flow of Credit to Households and Businesses</a>, Lorie K. Logan, April 14, 2020.</p>
<p><a name="footnote5"></a><sup>5</sup> </a> Includes repurchase agreements,  central bank liquidity swaps, primary and secondary credit, Primary Dealer  Credit Facility, Money Market Mutual Fund Liquidity Facility, Paycheck  Protection Program Liquidity Facility, and net portfolio holdings of the Commercial  Paper Funding Facility, Corporate Credit Facilities, Main Street Lending  Program, Municipal Liquidity Facility and Term Asset-Backed Securities Loan  Facility.</p>
<p><a name="footnote6"></a><sup>6</sup> For a review of how the Federal  Reserve&rsquo;s asset purchases impact reserves, see Ihrig, Jane, Lawrence Mize, and Gretchen  C. Weinbach (2017). <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/feds/files/2017099pap.pdf" target="_blank">How does the Fed adjust its Securities  Holdings and Who is Affected?</a>, Finance and Economics Discussion Series 2017-099.  Washington: Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, and Deborah  Leonard, Antoine Martin, and Simon M. Potter, <a href="http://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2017/07/how-the-fed-changes-the-size-of-its-balance-sheet.html" target="_blank">How the Fed Changes the Size of Its  Balance Sheet</a>,  Federal Reserve Bank of New York Liberty Street Economics (blog), July 10,  2017. </p>
<p><a name="footnote7"></a><sup>7</sup> Although aggregate reserve levels  reached historic highs, <a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/?g=wVqP" target="_blank">total bank reserves as a percent of total bank assets </a>were slightly lower than the August  2014 peak. Source: H.6 and H.8 data series. </p>
<p><a name="footnote8"></a><sup>8</sup> This includes the foreign repo pool,  which is a short-term liquid investment option available to foreign central  banks. Foreign repo pool usage has fallen slightly since March. </p>
<p><a name="footnote9"></a><sup>9</sup> For additional information, please see  the <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sm1081" target="_blank">U.S. Treasury&rsquo;s August 2020 Quarterly  Refunding Statement</a>. </p>
<p><a name="footnote10"></a><sup>10</sup> For more information on Treasury&rsquo;s  cash management policy, see the <a href="https://www.treasury.gov/press-center/press-releases/Pages/jl10045.aspx" target="_blank">U.S. Treasury&rsquo;s May 2015 Quarterly  Refunding Statement</a>. </p>
<p><a name="footnote11"></a><sup>11</sup> The Federal Reserve Banks pay interest  on required reserve balances (IORR) and on excess reserve balances (IOER) under  authority in Section 19 of the Federal Reserve Act. The Board of Governors has  prescribed rules governing the payment of interest by Federal Reserve Banks in  Regulation D. Since 2008, these two rates have been set at the same value. Prior  to 2008, there was no interest paid on reserves.</p>
<p><a name="footnote12"></a><sup>12</sup> For a perspective on the shape of the  reserve demand curve in an ample reserve environment see Afonso, Gara, Kyungmin  Kim, Antoine Martin, Ed Nosal, Simon Potter, and Sam Schulhofer-Wohl (2020). <a href="https://doi.org/10.17016/FEDS.2020.020" target="_blank">Monetary Policy Implementation with an Ample Supply of  Reserves</a>, Finance  and Economics Discussion Series 2020-020. Washington: Board of Governors of the  Federal Reserve System.</p>
<p><a name="footnote13"></a><sup>13</sup> Even in an ample reserves  environment, some banks, typically smaller institutions, pay rates above IOER  to meet liquidity shortfalls. This is reflected by the 99th  percentile of rates on federal funds transactions generally being above IOER. Some  banks may also be willing to pay rates slightly above IOER if borrowing from an  institution that has a more attractive runoff rate under the Liquidity Coverage  Ratio requirements. </p>
<p><a name="footnote14"></a><sup>14</sup> The SFOS is  a collection of qualitative and limited quantitative information about  liability management, the provision of financial services, and the functioning  of key financial markets from a selection of commercial banks, including U.S.  and foreign banking institutions. The 80 respondents of the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/other20201130a.htm" target="_blank">September 2020 SFOS</a> span a diverse set of foreign and  domestic banks in terms of business models and size.</p>
<p><a name="footnote15"></a><sup>15</sup> Though SFOS respondents did not rate  it as a top driver, another possible contributor is the temporary regulatory  relief measures, such as the temporary exemption of reserves and Treasury  holdings from the Supplementary Leverage Ratio (SLR) calculation.</p>
<p><a name="footnote16"></a><sup>16</sup> See Gara  Afonso, Marco Cipriani, Gabriele La Spada, and Will Riordan, <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2020/07/a-new-reserves-regime-covid-19-and-the-federal-reserve-balance-sheet.html" target="_blank">A New Reserves Regime? COVID-19 and  the Federal Reserve Balance Sheet</a>, Federal Reserve Bank of New York Liberty Street  Economics, July 7, 2020. During this period, the concentration of reserves also  increased (see Gara Afonso, Marco Cipriani, Steph Clampitt, Haitham Jendoubi,  Gabriele La Spada, and Will Riordan, <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2020/11/how-bank-reserves-are-distributed-matters-how-you-measure-their-distribution-matters-too.html" target="_blank">How Bank Reserves Are Distributed  Matters. How You Measure Their Distribution Matters Too.</a>, Federal Reserve Bank of  New York Liberty Street Economics, November 24, 2020). </p>
<p><a name="footnote17"></a><sup>17</sup> Foreign banking organizations  increased their reserve balances sharply for a brief period in March and April,  as shown in <a title="Chart" rel="facebox" href="#photo6">Figure 6</a>, as some borrowed more from their parent organizations,  which accessed dollar liquidity through the network of standing central bank  swap lines. See Nicola Cetorelli, Linda S. Goldberg, and Fabiola Ravazzolo, <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2020/06/how-fed-swap-lines-supported-the-us-corporate-credit-market-amid-covid-19-strains.html" target="_blank">How Fed Swap Lines Supported the U.S.  Corporate Credit Market amid COVID-19 Strains</a>, Federal Reserve Bank of New York Liberty Street  Economics, June 12, 2020.</p>
<p><a name="footnote18"></a><sup>18</sup> See Antoine Martin, James J. McAndrews, Ali  Palida, and David Skeie, <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2020/08/explaining-the-puzzling-behavior-of-short-term-money-market-rates.html" target="_blank">Explaining the  Puzzling Behavior of Short-Term Money Market Rates</a>, Federal Reserve Bank of New York Liberty Street Economics,  August 24, 2020. The updated Staff Report analysis indicated that $1 trillion  of additional reserves tends to reduce the effective federal funds rate by 8  basis points relative to the IOR rate, while an additional $1 trillion of  Treasury securities with under one year to maturity tends to increase the effective  federal funds rate by about 3 basis points. </p>
<p><a name="footnote19"></a><sup>19</sup> Net interest margins for U.S. banks reached  a 36-year low in the second quarter of 2020 according to data collected by the  Federal Financial Institutions Examination Council.</p>
<p><a name="footnote20"></a><sup>20</sup> The relative funding costs and  incentives for holding reserves were impacted by the widening of the FDIC&rsquo;s  assessment base and changes to the Basel III leverage ratio. FBOs were not  subject to the FDIC&rsquo;s fee nor did most have daily-averaged leverage ratios. For  further discussion, see Ayelen Banegas and Manjola Tase (2016). <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econresdata/feds/2016/files/2016079pap.pdf" target="_blank">Reserve Balances, the Federal Funds  Market and Arbitrage in the New Regulatory Framework</a>, FEDS Notes, Board of Governors of  the Federal Reserve System, September 1, 2016. </p>
<p><a name="footnote21"></a><sup>21</sup> The Desk  also conducts daily, large-scale overnight repo operations at a fixed rate five  basis points above IOER. These operations could address any unexpected firmness  in rates associated with shocks to the financial system or reserves. </p>
<p><a name="footnote22"></a><sup>22</sup> From  late-2013 to 2015, the Desk conducted the ON RRP as a test operation to  facilitate policy rate control. For further discussion of these test operations  and the design of the ON RRP, see Josh Frost, Lorie Logan, Antoine Martin,  Patrick McCabe, Fabio Natalucci and Julie Remache (2015). <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/econresdata/feds/2015/files/2015010pap.pdf" target="_blank">Overnight RRP Operations as a Monetary  Policy Tool: Some Design Considerations</a>, FEDS Notes, Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve  System, February 19, 2015. Since the overall limit on the ON RRP size was  lifted in December 2015, the effective federal funds rate has only fallen below  the ON RRP rate once. On December 31, 2015 the effective rate was 20 basis  points while the ON RRP rate and the bottom of the target range were 25 basis  points.</p>
<p><a name="footnote23"></a><sup>23</sup> Federal Home Loan Banks are major  lenders in the federal funds market and can also leave balances in their  accounts at the Federal Reserve which earn zero percent.&nbsp;</p>    </div>
         

</div>

</div>
